[{:db/id 17592186362373 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "Interpret the positive quantity calculated below as the magnitude of the rate of change in the direction of fastest decrease.\n\nAt a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSigned-rate note: $\\nabla f(1,3)=(9,1)$ and the unit direction of fastest decrease is $\\mathbf u=(-9,-1)/\\sqrt{82}$. Therefore $D_{\\mathbf u}f(1,3)=\\nabla f(1,3)\\cdot\\mathbf u=-\\sqrt{82}$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{82}$, which is the positive quantity requested in the clarified study question." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
